    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2176.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 619,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on Earth.** It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at its chemistry, its states, its importance to Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemistry of Water\n* **Chemical Formula:** **$H_2O$** \n  This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water has a unique chemical structure (it is a \"polar\" molecule, meaning the oxygen side has a slight negative charge and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge). Because of this, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid—from salt and sugar to minerals and gases. This is why it is called the universal solvent.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n* **Liquid:** The standard form found in oceans, rivers, and rain.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). *Unique property:* Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity:** Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and groundwater.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a continuous cycle—evaporating from the oceans into the clouds, falling back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flowing back to the oceans via rivers.\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nWater is the defining prerequisite for life as we know it:\n* **Biological Roles:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. Water regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and acts as the medium for all cellular chemical reactions.\n* **Ecosystems:** Aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems rely entirely on water to sustain flora and fauna.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule ($H_2O$) with extraordinary properties. It shapes our planet's geography, drives our weather and climate, and acts as the literal lifeblood of every living organism."
